# 6. AUTOMATIC CALENDAR SYNCHRONIZATION

This is the central integration mechanism.

The calendar must update automatically when information changes elsewhere in the website.

It must not depend on the farmer manually refreshing the page.

## 6.1 Event-driven synchronization

Implement an internal domain-event mechanism using the project's existing architecture.

If a message broker or event system already exists, reuse it.

Otherwise, a transactional database outbox pattern is appropriate.

Examples of internal domain events:

```text
FarmUpdated
FieldUpdated

RotationPlanCreated
RotationPlanUpdated
RotationPlanApproved

CropStageRecalculated
PlantingDateChanged
HarvestDateChanged

IrrigationRecommendationCreated
IrrigationRecommendationUpdated
IrrigationCompleted

SoilTestScheduled
SoilTestCompleted

ScoutingScheduled
ScoutingCompleted

FertilizerRecommendationUpdated

AnalysisScheduled
AnalysisCompleted
AnalysisMarkedStale

TaskCreated
TaskUpdated
TaskCompleted
TaskDeferred
TaskRescheduled
TaskCancelled

ObservationRecorded
AlertCreated
AlertUpdated
```

These are event names for the integration contract, not instructions to duplicate existing domain models.

## 6.2 Synchronization workflow

Example: the farmer changes a planting date in the Rotation Planner.

1. Rotation Planner submits its update.
2. Backend validates the change.
3. Rotation service saves a new plan version.
4. Database transaction commits.
5. A domain event is recorded in the transactional outbox.
6. The event publisher delivers it.
7. Calendar projection dependencies are invalidated.
8. Affected planned events are regenerated.
9. Relevant crop-stage and rotation calculations are requested through their owning services.
10. Affected Deep Analysis records are marked stale where appropriate.
11. The calendar's affected date range is refreshed.
12. Connected browser sessions receive a synchronization notification.
13. The farmer sees the updated calendar.

The original plan must not be modified by the calendar.

## 6.3 Outbox pattern

Avoid publishing a domain event before the associated database transaction has successfully committed.

Otherwise, the calendar could receive an update for a change that was never saved.

Recommended structure:

```text
Database transaction
    |
    +-- Update canonical record
    |
    +-- Insert outbox event
    |
    +-- Commit
           |
           v
    Background event publisher
           |
           v
    Calendar projection invalidation
```

The publisher must retry failed deliveries.

Consumers must be idempotent.

Record event IDs and processing status to prevent repeated processing.

## 6.4 Real-time frontend updates

If the project supports WebSockets or Server-Sent Events, use the existing real-time mechanism.

Otherwise, implement an appropriate lightweight refresh mechanism.

When a relevant update arrives:

- Determine the affected farm.
- Determine the affected field.
- Determine the affected date range.
- Refresh only the affected calendar data.
- Preserve the selected date.
- Preserve the selected month.
- Preserve unrelated filters.
- Update Today's Activities if its contents changed.
- Update Next 7 Days if a relevant event changed.

Do not reload the entire website after every event.

## 6.5 Fallback synchronization

Real-time delivery is not guaranteed.

Implement:
- Refresh on browser focus.
- Refresh after relevant frontend mutations.
- Periodic lightweight synchronization when necessary.
- Version or updated-at checks.
- Recovery after temporary network disconnection.

The page must always be able to retrieve a fresh calendar from the canonical backend.

